{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target asks to argue for or against the claim that a decentralized network of small services is more resilient to correlated failure than one large service with strictly more internal redundancy, and to identify evidence distinguishing truly independent from merely uncorrelated failures. SRC_001 addresses only the evidence-distinguishing half: it supplies an intervention test for shared causes. It supplies no argument, mechanism, or evidence bearing on the comparative resilience claim — the 'for or against' question about decentralized vs. large-redundant services is never engaged. Under a covering-principle reading, the problem has two limbs and the target covers only one, so it fails the problem as stated.","counterexample":null,"target_alias":"SRC_001"}]}